WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.290
(cheerful music)

00:00:01.290 --> 00:00:02.582
- [Voiceover] Blakely has
the most difficult job

00:00:02.582 --> 00:00:05.969
in Cincinnati Zoo, he has to
play with babies all the time.

00:00:05.969 --> 00:00:07.241
(squeaking)

00:00:07.241 --> 00:00:08.074
(hissing)

00:00:08.074 --> 00:00:10.408
He's never shown any
aggression or snapped.

00:00:10.408 --> 00:00:13.547
If he gets upset, kinda
like any mom would,

00:00:13.547 --> 00:00:16.800
he just leaves the room. (laughing)

00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:19.825
- [Voiceover] You are so cute!

00:00:19.825 --> 00:00:20.967
- My name is Dawn Strasser, and we're

00:00:20.967 --> 00:00:23.508
at the Cincinnati Zoo and Botanical Garden

00:00:23.508 --> 00:00:26.378
Blakely is a five year
old Australian Shepherd.

00:00:26.378 --> 00:00:27.406
We got him from a rescue.

00:00:27.406 --> 00:00:31.247
And I use him in the nursery
as a nanny, or companion dog.

00:00:31.247 --> 00:00:33.562
This is about you, and
you're taking a nap!

00:00:33.562 --> 00:00:37.573
Blakely's here for the main
purpose of teaching the babies

00:00:37.573 --> 00:00:39.574
the correct animal cues as they grow up,

00:00:39.574 --> 00:00:42.574
so when they are introduced
back to their own kind,

00:00:42.574 --> 00:00:44.576
they know what appropriate behavior is.

00:00:44.576 --> 00:00:46.805
He teaches them how to
play, how to interact.

00:00:46.805 --> 00:00:51.267
I can tell them, no don't
bite me, but I'm not an animal

00:00:51.267 --> 00:00:54.847
and I don't give the
same cues that he can.

00:00:54.847 --> 00:00:57.353
So we make a great team.

00:00:57.353 --> 00:01:00.103
(cheerful music)

00:01:01.097 --> 00:01:04.014
- [Voiceover] Smile for the camera!

00:01:05.856 --> 00:01:08.825
- [Dawn] Blakely has worked
with cheetahs, ocelots,

00:01:08.825 --> 00:01:11.908
a takin, bat-eared foxes, a warthog,

00:01:13.498 --> 00:01:15.498
wallabies, to name a few.

00:01:16.583 --> 00:01:20.132
He's working with four
baby cheetahs right now.

00:01:20.132 --> 00:01:22.041
It's been a long seven weeks for him

00:01:22.041 --> 00:01:25.018
with these little cheetah
cubs, and he's, I think, tired.

00:01:25.018 --> 00:01:27.334
He's on alert for 24 hours a day.

00:01:27.334 --> 00:01:29.757
If they start making too much
noise, he'll come and get us.

00:01:29.757 --> 00:01:31.594
Like, something's not right,
something's not right.

00:01:31.594 --> 00:01:34.221
He's kinda like a second
set of eyes for us, too.

00:01:34.221 --> 00:01:36.597
A lot of times they still
recognize each other,

00:01:36.597 --> 00:01:39.455
like Dale the takin he raised,
when they see each other,

00:01:39.455 --> 00:01:42.360
Dale'll still run up in
front and grunt at him,

00:01:42.360 --> 00:01:45.075
and he'll kinda jump up on
the wall and it’s kinda like,

00:01:45.075 --> 00:01:47.047
hey, how ya doin'?

00:01:47.047 --> 00:01:48.350
- He's beautiful.

00:01:48.350 --> 00:01:50.671
- [Dawn] Thank you!

00:01:50.671 --> 00:01:52.610
Yeah, well, I like to
think I have an office,

00:01:52.610 --> 00:01:55.027
but I'm pretty sure it's his.

00:01:59.935 --> 00:02:03.268
(light cheerful music)

